  <h2>What makes a waiver unconditional</h2>
  <p>
    An unconditional waiver states that you have been paid and releases your rights for that payment
    with no strings attached. It is simpler for the owner and the lender to rely on, which is why
    they ask for it — and riskier for you, because it takes effect whether or not the money is good.
    The forms here print a boxed notice to the claimant saying exactly that, so nobody signs the
    wrong form by accident.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you have not been paid yet, you want the
    <a href="/templates/conditional-lien-waiver/">conditional lien waiver</a> instead. The
    <a href="/templates/lien-waiver/">four-form lien waiver set</a> has both kinds together, and our guide
    to <a href="/blog/construction-lien-waivers-explained/"
      >how conditional and unconditional lien waivers work</a
    > walks through when each one changes hands on a job.
  </p>

  <h2>Progress or final</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th></th><th>Unconditional progress (form 1)</th><th>Unconditional final (form 2)</th></tr
      >
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr
        ><td><strong>Covers</strong></td><td
          >One payment received, for work through the through date</td
        ><td>The whole job, retention included</td></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td><strong>Keeps</strong></td><td
          >Retention, pending change orders, disputed items, later work</td
        ><td>Only the disputed claims you list</td></tr
      >
      <tr
        ><td><strong>Sign when</strong></td><td>That payment has cleared</td><td
          >The final payment, retention included, has cleared</td
        ></tr
      >
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Statutory-form states</h2>
  <p>
    At least twelve states — California, Texas, Arizona, Nevada, Utah, Georgia, Florida,
    Massachusetts, Michigan, Missouri, Mississippi and Wyoming — prescribe the wording of lien
    waivers by statute; the <a href="/templates/lien-waiver/">lien waiver forms page</a> lists the statute
    for each. Florida’s progress and final forms are in § 713.20(4) and (5), and § 713.20(6) says a person
    may not require a lienor to furnish a different form. In those states, use the statutory form rather
    than this general one. On a Florida job, expect the owner to ask for waivers from every sub and supplier
    who served a <a href="/templates/notice-to-owner/">notice to owner</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>A worked example</h2>
  <p>
    The completed example follows the pay application sample. Application no. 3 billed August 2026
    with a current payment due of $122,557.50; the conditional waiver went with the application on
    September 3. When the payment cleared, the subcontractor signed the unconditional progress
    waiver for the same $122,557.50 through August 31, dated October 2, still carving out $28,680.50
    of retention and a $5,784.50 change order that had not been approved when the waiver was
    drafted. The unconditional final waiver is dated January 8, 2027, after the retention release of
    $49,735.45 cleared, with no disputed claims.
  </p>

  <h2>Why owners and lenders ask for it</h2>
  <p>
    A conditional waiver proves you will release your rights once paid; an unconditional waiver
    proves you were paid. Construction lenders and title companies usually want the unconditional
    waiver for the previous payment before they release the next draw, because it closes the loop on
    money that has already gone out. That is why the normal rhythm on a monthly job is a conditional
    waiver for this month’s application and an unconditional waiver for last month’s payment, sent
    together.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before you sign, reconcile the payment against your billing: the amount received, the
    application it pays, and the retention still held. If the payment was short, do not sign an
    unconditional waiver for the full amount billed — sign for what you received and list the
    shortfall as a disputed amount in the exceptions. At closeout, the unconditional final waiver
    usually sits in the package with the <a href="/templates/certificate-of-completion/"
      >certificate of substantial completion</a
    > and your warranties, but it should not be signed until the final payment clears.
  </p>

  <h2>Common mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Signing when the check arrives instead of when it clears.</li>
    <li>Signing an unconditional final waiver before the retention is paid.</li>
    <li>Leaving the exceptions blank on a progress waiver.</li>
    <li>Using a general form in a statutory-form state.</li>
    <li>Not getting matching waivers from your own subs and suppliers after you pay them.</li>
  </ul>
